title: Text search
pid: b3f21f7e-ab6e-48ba-af19-749c21344b41
body: |2-
    - Request: =((request . "text search") (terms . "SEARCH_TERMS")
      (regex . "BOOL") (body . "BOOL") (operators . "BOOL"))=, plus
      optional =(overPrivateText-telescopes . "include")= or
      =(overPrivateText-telescopes . "exclude")=.
      - Search always returns every match. Prominent nodes (a root, a member of a
        cycle, a node linked to, a node that had an ID before import, or a
        multiply contained node; 'docs/glossary.org', "prominent") are ranked
        higher, via their prominence multiplier.
      - =regex=, =body=, =operators= are optional; each defaults to "false".
        - "regex=true": interpret the query as a per-token regex; a RegexQuery is built directly and the QueryParser is bypassed.
        - "body=true": also search node bodies (titles are always searched).
        - "operators=true": preserve Tantivy phrase and operator syntax (AND / OR / phrase / +foo / -bar / grouping / field:). Intra-word operator chars still get escaped heuristically so C++ etc. remain findable. In regex mode, the query is parsed as a boolean expression over the per-token regexes: AND / OR / NOT / +foo / -bar, conventional precedence (NOT > AND > OR, bare adjacency = OR), and grouping via parens that stand alone between whitespace (a paren attached to a pattern is regex syntax within that piece). Malformed operator syntax is an error; phrase syntax does not apply. Detailed in docs/COMMANDS.org.
      - The skgrepo restriction is applied before grouping, ranking,
        alias/title selection, and display truncation. Restricted-skgrepo
        documents do not influence result order or which title/alias is
        shown for an unrestricted result.
      - =overPrivateText-telescopes= is normally absent on the first request. Under
        a restricted skgrepo-set, if the search index contains any telescope
        whose selected title or body lies below home, the server does no
        query and responds with LP
        =((response-type overPrivateText-telescope-confirmation)
        (operation text-search) (pids ()) (prompt "..."))=. The empty PID
        list is deliberate: a pre-query decision must not disclose which
        overPrivateText node might match. The retry uses ="include"= or ="exclude"=.
        Exclusion is applied inside the Tantivy query, before matching,
        and to what the enrichment draws, after it: the occurrences of
        overPrivateText nodes there become tombstones, and childless
        tombstones are pruned. The choice
        is request-scoped. An unrestricted connection needs no choice
        and returns a warning if the results include overPrivateText
        telescopes.

    - Response: one LP =((response-type search-results) (content
      "ORG") (warnings ("..." ...)))=, whose content is the search
      buffer: each hit, and what the enrichment draws under it. A
      no-match or error response has the same response type and
      a single =content= field, a message to the user. (The
      overPrivateText challenge above is sent instead of it.) No other
      message follows; the search buffer is an ordinary view from
      then on, and closing it is an ordinary =close view= of
      =search:TERMS=.
      - The hits are the roots of the search buffer, ordinary
        write-protected occurrences (not special non-vognode kinds),
        best first. Each shows its node's title.
      - The search draws the enrichment by a refresh of a stub built
        from the hits ('build_search_viewforest', then
        'draw_search_buffer'), the same code every view is drawn
        with: each hit asks for its containers folder ('(folder
        containers)'), and holds an empty forks folder when that
        folder's goal has members, and an empty alias folder when an
        alias of its node matched; the refresh
        fills the folders and draws each containers folder member's
        containerward chain.
      - Under each hit hang its containers folder, whose members carry
        the containerward chain; and its forks folder, flat, as every
        forks folder is: the visible nodes it forks, then those that
        fork it, then its other mirrors (those it hides aside), each
        member's chain in a containers folder under it, and no member
        showing a forks folder of its own (see "View requests"). A
        folder with no member is not drawn. The
        members are write-protected. Everything under a hit is folded,
        so that the hit shows collapsed; but a hit found through an
        alias shows its folders, each collapsed except its alias
        folder, which shows all of the node's aliases, the matching one
        among them. Among hits that are mirrors of one another
        (=docs/mirrors.org=, "Mirrors"), the one the user's own skgrepo
        list ranks first stands: the list of the perspective of the
        config's first owned skgrepo, the most public, then the others
        in declaration order, so that among owned hits the most public
        stands; several in one skgrepo tie, and the least pid
        stands. If it is owned, every other
        hit among them is suppressed at the top level
        ('suppressed_result_gnids'), and reappears in the one forks
        folder of the hit standing, which lists every mirror of it, one
        it forks or that forks it and one joined to it only through
        other notes (a sibling fork, say) alike; one the standing hit
        hides stands in that folder's folder of what it hides outside
        its fork partners. A foreign hit suppresses nothing.
      - The search draws the enrichment under the same
        skgrepo restriction as it searches under. The containerward
        chain stops before restricted containers.
      - The search takes no hit cap and no node budget. The refresh's
        node budget exists, and the stub could be given one; that is
        left unmade.
      - Note: the search once answered in two phases: the hits at once,
        then, after the server asked for a snapshot of the search
        buffer, the enrichment under them. That protocol showed the hits a
        tenth to a quarter of a second earlier for a search of hundreds
        of hits, and was removed for its complexity
        ('TODO/simplify-refresh/bench-search-enrichment.org' holds the
        measurements).
